 A Better Learning Roadmap

Instead of jumping straight into automation tools, build your knowledge step by step:

📌 1. Understand Software Development

- SDLC & STLC
- Frontend vs Backend
- Client-Server Architecture
- APIs, Databases, HTTP Requests & Responses

📌 2. Learn One Programming Language Well
Focus on concepts like:

- Variables & Data Types
- Conditions & Loops
- Functions
- Arrays/Lists
- Objects & Classes
- Exception Handling
- Asynchronous Programming (for JavaScript)

📌 3. Learn Git & Version Control
Understand repositories, commits, branches, merging, and pull requests.

📌 4. Learn Web Technologies
HTML, CSS, JavaScript basics, the DOM, browser DevTools, and locators (XPath/CSS Selectors).

📌 5. Master API Testing
Learn REST APIs, JSON, authentication, headers, request methods, and practice with Postman.

📌 6. Learn an Automation Framework
Now tools like Playwright, Selenium, Cypress, or Appium will make much more sense because you'll understand why the code works.

📌 7. Learn Framework Design & CI/CD
Understand Page Object Model (POM), reporting, reusable components, GitHub Actions, Jenkins, or other CI/CD tools.

📌 8. Learn Basic SQL
A good Automation Engineer should be comfortable querying and validating data from a database.

💡 Final Thoughts

Test automation is not about memorizing scripts—it's about applying software engineering principles to testing.

Frameworks and tools will continue to evolve, but strong fundamentals will always remain relevant.

So, if you're just starting your automation journey, don't rush to master a tool.

Master the fundamentals first, and every tool becomes easier. 💯
